From 60aa40047b01570c7baf77e8c202e55ce80a964a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 03:30:16 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] drm/amd/display: the 4K modes with an HDMI VIC default to
 limited RGB too

linux-9970 (5342f7d98a26 of amd-staging-drm-next, backported) makes
Broadcast RGB "Automatic" follow the CTA-861 default range: limited on a
CTA mode of an HDMI sink, which rgb_output_is_limited_range() tells by
dc_crtc_timing.vic > 1. That field is the VIC of the AVI InfoFrame
(fill_stream_properties_from_drm_display_mode() copies
avi_frame.video_code), and DRM sends VIC 0 there for 3840x2160 at 24, 25
and 30 Hz and 4096x2160 at 24 Hz: those go out with their HDMI VIC in the
HDMI vendor InfoFrame (drm_mode_cea_vic() and drm_mode_hdmi_vic(), on any
sink with an HDMI vendor block), which amdgpu keeps in
dc_crtc_timing.hdmi_vic. So on a sink that cannot select the range (no
QS), Automatic stayed full range with the default quantization at the
film rates, which the sink takes as limited (crushed shadows, clipped
highlights), while 2160p50 and 60 got limited range. The commit of AMD
has the same gap.

A mode is now a CTA mode when the InfoFrames name it as one: a VIC above
1 in the AVI InfoFrame, or an HDMI VIC. The three callers of
get_output_color_space() pass a timing that carries hdmi_vic (the
stream's own, or timing_out after it is set). i915 and the HDMI state
helper get limited range at those modes too (drm_default_rgb_quant_range()
matches the mode against the CTA table). They differ in one case that
this does not change: a VIC above 64 that an HDMI 1.4 sink does not list
goes out as VIC 0 without an HDMI VIC, a format the InfoFrames do not
name as CTA, and amdgpu keeps full range there, as it says.

Nothing changes for a sink that can select the range (linux-9971), for
Full and Limited, for YCbCr, for DisplayPort and DVI, or for the Dolby
Vision tunnel (linux-9910 sets hdmi_vic to 0 and the AVI VIC to the CTA
one, and Full).

Tested on the host with the decision code cut out of amdgpu_dm.c: an
HDMI sink without QS at VIC 0 with HDMI VIC 1 to 4 now gets limited
range, and every other case is as before; the rule without this change
fails those cases, and taking the HDMI VIC out of the rule again is caught.
---
 drivers/gpu/drm/amd/display/amdgpu_dm/amdgpu_dm.c | 8 +++++++-
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/display/amdgpu_dm/amdgpu_dm.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/display/amdgpu_dm/amdgpu_dm.c
index 4558c83..42f5fe1 100644
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/display/amdgpu_dm/amdgpu_dm.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/display/amdgpu_dm/amdgpu_dm.c
@@ -7006,6 +7006,11 @@ get_aspect_ratio(const struct drm_display_mode *mode_in)
  * CTA-861 5.1: RGB video formats default to limited range except 640x480 (VIC 1).
  * With Broadcast RGB left at Automatic follow that default, as i915 does.
  *
+ * Hades: a CTA mode is one that the InfoFrames name as one: a VIC above 1 in the AVI
+ * InfoFrame, or an HDMI VIC in the HDMI vendor InfoFrame. DRM names 3840x2160 at 24, 25 and
+ * 30 Hz and 4096x2160 at 24 Hz with their HDMI VIC and VIC 0 in the AVI InfoFrame
+ * (drm_mode_cea_vic()), and they default to limited range like any other CTA mode.
+ *
  * Hades: except on a sink that can select the RGB range (QS in its Video Capability Data
  * Block): there Automatic stays full range, and the AVI InfoFrame says so (Q = full,
  * set_avi_info_frame() with stream->qs_bit), as before. That signal is consistent, and the
@@ -7022,7 +7027,8 @@ static bool rgb_output_is_limited_range(const struct dc_crtc_timing *dc_crtc_tim
 	case DRM_HDMI_BROADCAST_RGB_LIMITED:
 		return true;
 	default:
-		return info->is_hdmi && dc_crtc_timing->vic > 1 &&
+		return info->is_hdmi &&
+		       (dc_crtc_timing->vic > 1 || dc_crtc_timing->hdmi_vic) &&
 		       !info->rgb_quant_range_selectable;
 	}
 }
